<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">69 CE</h1><p class="ref-lede">69 CE in world history: 7 events and 2 deaths. 139 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/48">48 CE</a><a href="/years/62">62 CE</a><a href="/years/66">66 CE</a><a href="/years/68">68 CE</a><a href="/years/69" aria-current="page">69 CE</a><a href="/years/70">70 CE</a><a href="/years/79">79 CE</a><a href="/years/142">142 CE</a><a href="/years/161">161 CE</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/galba" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-10">10 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Galba adopts Piso Licinianus as his heir</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Galba</a> adopted the nobleman Lucius Calpurnius Piso Frugi Licinianus, passing over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a>, who had expected to be adopted instead. Otho had both men murdered and became the second emperor of the Year of the Four Emperors.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Galba</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-15">15 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Galba murdered in the Roman Forum</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Praetorians loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a> killed the elderly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Galba</a> after just seven months as emperor. Otho, then Vitellius and finally Vespasian took power in the turmoil known as the Year of the Four Emperors.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Vitellius's army defeats Otho at Bedriacum</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Troops loyal to Vitellius beat Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a>'s forces near Cremona in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Otho took his own life two days later, but Vitellius himself fell before the year was out.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/otho" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otho.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-16">16 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Emperor Otho takes his own life after his defeat at Bedriacum</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a>, the second emperor of the Year of the Four Emperors, ended his three-month reign by suicide after Vitellius defeated him at the Battle of Bedriacum. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> recognised Vitellius as emperor on 19 April.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otho">Otho</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-21">21 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Senate recognises Vespasian as emperor</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The day after Vitellius was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the Senate granted Vespasian the powers of emperor, ending the Year of the Four Emperors. His Flavian dynasty restored stability and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colosseum">Colosseum</a>.</p></div></li></ol></section>
